1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,760
Well, good morning, Canvas Church.

2
00:00:08,760 --> 00:00:11,680
Thanks so much for joining us.

3
00:00:11,680 --> 00:00:13,540
Thanks for worshiping with us this morning.

4
00:00:13,540 --> 00:00:17,220
Donna, thanks for preparing on our hearts.

5
00:00:17,220 --> 00:00:22,760
Super thankful for all that God has done in Christ for us.

6
00:00:22,760 --> 00:00:28,520
This morning, we have one week between the end of our series through the book of James.

7
00:00:28,520 --> 00:00:29,700
Let me introduce myself first.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,500
For those of you that don't know me yet, if you're new here, my name is Ryan.

9
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:33,740
I'm one of the pastors here.

10
00:00:33,740 --> 00:00:35,500
I'm so glad that you have joined us.

11
00:00:35,500 --> 00:00:38,700
Last week, we finished up a series on the book of James.

12
00:00:38,700 --> 00:00:43,240
Dave did an excellently long job.

13
00:00:43,240 --> 00:00:46,320
He told me I owe you guys a few minutes, so I'm going to try and end.

14
00:00:46,320 --> 00:00:47,680
I think he's watching online.

15
00:00:47,680 --> 00:00:48,360
Dave, I love you.

16
00:00:48,360 --> 00:00:55,760
We finished up the series through the book of James, and we have our outdoor service next Sunday.

17
00:00:55,760 --> 00:01:02,280
After that, we're starting a series through the spiritual fruits that we're calling Summer Fruit because we're creative and catchy.

18
00:01:02,280 --> 00:01:05,380
That's the only reason we called it that.

19
00:01:05,380 --> 00:01:06,740
No, but I'm excited about that series.

20
00:01:06,740 --> 00:01:12,500
I had one week in between the end of James and our series coming up on spiritual fruits.

21
00:01:13,120 --> 00:01:24,480
If I'm honest, stand-alone messages, I really struggle with having freedom to pick what I'm going to talk about because as a communicator of the Scriptures,

22
00:01:24,480 --> 00:01:36,120
I know that there's a temptation to pull out my soapbox and stand on the things that are important to me and talk about the things that I want to talk about rather than letting God speak to us.

23
00:01:36,120 --> 00:01:41,360
And that's why I love talking through books of the Bible, talking through chapters of the Bible, because we kind of let God be the primary communicator.

24
00:01:41,360 --> 00:01:48,080
And so I was really wrestling this week of like, man, don't insert yourself into the pulpit this week.

25
00:01:48,080 --> 00:01:51,580
Like, what do we need to hear from God?

26
00:01:51,580 --> 00:02:02,360
And as I was thinking about the spiritual fruits series, really there was one word, one topic that isn't going to be covered in the spiritual fruit series that I think is really crucial,

27
00:02:02,360 --> 00:02:06,000
really central to the Christian faith, and that word is humility.

28
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:14,680
And so after some prayer and thinking, knowing I only had one week, what is something that's primary, central to the Christian faith,

29
00:02:14,680 --> 00:02:16,460
something that we really need to hear about?

30
00:02:16,460 --> 00:02:21,140
And I thought about the word humility, which led me to this passage in Philippians chapter 2,

31
00:02:21,140 --> 00:02:27,760
which some of you that grew up in church are probably familiar with a famous passage about the humility of Jesus to come to earth,

32
00:02:27,760 --> 00:02:33,940
to lay down his life, to sacrificially serve his creation, to draw us to himself.

33
00:02:33,940 --> 00:02:36,780
And so that's where we're going to spend our time this morning.

34
00:02:36,780 --> 00:02:40,880
And the main idea that I'm drawing from that text is this.

35
00:02:40,880 --> 00:02:50,380
I believe that Paul is going to share with us from that text that the successful Christian life and church are assembled with an attitude of humility.

36
00:02:50,380 --> 00:02:56,620
The successful Christian life and church are assembled with the attitude of humility.

37
00:02:56,620 --> 00:02:58,460
And I chose these words intentionally.

38
00:02:58,460 --> 00:03:02,900
I chose the word successful, and I chose the word assembled on purpose.

39
00:03:02,900 --> 00:03:04,600
And we'll get to assembled in a little bit.

40
00:03:04,600 --> 00:03:12,880
But the reason I chose the word successful is because the book of Philippians is pretty unique compared to some of Paul's other letters in the Bible.

41
00:03:12,880 --> 00:03:16,960
Honestly, most of Philippians is pretty encouraging and cheerful.

42
00:03:16,960 --> 00:03:20,500
A lot of theologians call the book of Philippians the book of joy.

43
00:03:20,500 --> 00:03:23,120
Paul's just, like, pumped about the church at Philippi.

44
00:03:23,220 --> 00:03:25,580
He's pumped about the Christians at Philippi.

45
00:03:25,580 --> 00:03:29,060
And if you're familiar with the Scriptures at all, you know a lot of Paul's letters.

46
00:03:29,060 --> 00:03:32,640
He's kind of grumpy and calling out people for their sin, right?

47
00:03:32,640 --> 00:03:35,580
He's like, hey, you guys are messing up here, here, here.

48
00:03:35,580 --> 00:03:36,720
Like, here's what you need to correct.

49
00:03:36,720 --> 00:03:37,660
Here's what you need to change.

50
00:03:37,660 --> 00:03:39,160
Here's how you can get better.

51
00:03:39,160 --> 00:03:51,680
And really, Paul doesn't do much of that at all in the book of Philippians, which kind of leads us to believe that the Philippian church was actually a pretty healthy church full of pretty healthy believers.

52
00:03:51,680 --> 00:03:53,640
Like, they were getting it right.

53
00:03:53,640 --> 00:04:02,220
And Paul was encouraged by the health of the individual believers, their relationship with Jesus, the success that they were having as a church.

54
00:04:02,640 --> 00:04:08,520
And so the passage we're going to study this morning is the central passage of the book of Philippians.

55
00:04:08,520 --> 00:04:17,840
And I think what Paul is doing is he's saying, listen, the reason that you're so successful as individual Christians and as a church is because of your humility.

56
00:04:18,220 --> 00:04:27,880
I think he's reminding them to stay focused on this attitude that has allowed them to have the successful life and church that they've had up until this point.

57
00:04:27,880 --> 00:04:29,300
They've had an attitude of humility.

58
00:04:29,300 --> 00:04:39,140
But we all know, if we've been human long enough, we know that if we start to have success in anything, oftentimes we lose sight of the reason we had our success to be human.

59
00:04:39,680 --> 00:04:50,940
We could lose sight of the fact that it's actually an attitude of humility and sacrificial service that got us to where we are, and we might start to ad-lib or take control of our success for ourselves.

60
00:04:50,940 --> 00:05:00,760
And so I think Paul is centering his letter on the attitude of Christ's humility to remind the Philippian church to stay focused on the attitude of humility.

61
00:05:01,100 --> 00:05:09,200
And I think we can kind of take that same charge for ourselves and say, man, if we're going to succeed in a life of following Jesus,

62
00:05:09,200 --> 00:05:17,760
and if we're going to be a church that's successful in demonstrating the attitudes and love of Jesus to our city and to a watching world,

63
00:05:17,760 --> 00:05:23,520
humility has to be at the primary point of our lives.

64
00:05:23,520 --> 00:05:28,880
Let's not lose sight of what true Christian success looks like.

65
00:05:28,880 --> 00:05:37,260
And so I was thinking of a way to illustrate this, of this idea of having success and then losing sight of what made you successful to begin with.

66
00:05:37,260 --> 00:05:41,700
The Internet broke a few years ago with IHOP.

67
00:05:41,700 --> 00:05:46,660
IHOP launched this thing on Twitter where they decided they were going to change their name to IHOB.

68
00:05:46,660 --> 00:05:47,920
Does anybody remember this?

69
00:05:47,920 --> 00:05:50,120
A few people remember this.

70
00:05:50,120 --> 00:05:55,080
Twitter was, like, broken for a while because they didn't say anything about why they changed it to IHOB.

71
00:05:55,080 --> 00:05:56,420
They just, like, changed it to IHOB.

72
00:05:56,420 --> 00:05:57,460
And so then Twitter went nuts.

73
00:05:57,460 --> 00:05:59,900
Everybody was like, why is IHOB IHOB?

74
00:05:59,900 --> 00:06:01,100
This doesn't make any sense.

75
00:06:01,100 --> 00:06:07,840
And they launched this massive marketing campaign where they said that they were going to start doing burgers.

76
00:06:07,840 --> 00:06:14,160
The International House of Pancake was going to transition from breakfast to start making burgers.

77
00:06:14,420 --> 00:06:21,880
And for those of you that are familiar with Twitter at all, you know that the Wendy's Twitter account is a troll account, right?

78
00:06:21,880 --> 00:06:25,000
The Wendy's account loves to make fun of other businesses.

79
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:32,660
And so once IHOB announced that they were going to have burgers, I think I have a picture of it on the screen, maybe.

80
00:06:32,660 --> 00:06:38,500
Wendy's tweeted, not really afraid of burgers from a place that decided pancakes were too hard.

81
00:06:40,120 --> 00:06:50,620
And so this is a classic example of a company forgetting what made them successful, like losing sight of the thing that brought them the success that they had.

82
00:06:50,620 --> 00:06:52,900
Their name was literally pancakes, right?

83
00:06:52,900 --> 00:06:56,000
Like breakfast was the thing that they were known for.

84
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:01,940
But maybe they got a little bit too far out from their skis and decided they were going to try to do something different.

85
00:07:01,940 --> 00:07:15,320
And I think if we're honest with ourselves, whenever we have success in anything, but specifically in our relationship with Jesus, success in the Christian life always comes from being close to Jesus.

86
00:07:15,320 --> 00:07:21,300
Always comes from faith and trust in Jesus, allowing through the Holy Spirit to work out from us.

87
00:07:21,300 --> 00:07:23,340
Like we're not successful because of anything we do.

88
00:07:23,340 --> 00:07:25,880
We're successful because of God working through us.

89
00:07:25,880 --> 00:07:30,980
But because we're human beings, sinful, fallen, broken human beings, oftentimes we see that success.

90
00:07:30,980 --> 00:07:33,980
We see God working through us, producing fruit and doing things.

91
00:07:33,980 --> 00:07:41,660
And then we, because we're humans, maybe take hold of the reins for a little bit and say, man, I'm pretty good at this Christian thing.

92
00:07:41,660 --> 00:07:46,740
I'm pretty good at this ministry thing, whatever it might look like.

93
00:07:46,800 --> 00:07:51,240
And Paul is saying, you're doing great, keep it up.

94
00:07:51,240 --> 00:07:53,720
And the way that you keep it up is through humility.

95
00:07:53,720 --> 00:07:58,460
You're looking to Jesus, continually taking your eyes back to Jesus.

96
00:07:58,460 --> 00:08:06,060
There was another church that struggled to do this in Revelation, the church of Laodicea, this famous passage about the lukewarm church.

97
00:08:06,060 --> 00:08:08,960
And John says, why are they lukewarm?

98
00:08:08,960 --> 00:08:11,240
Because you forgot your former love.

99
00:08:11,240 --> 00:08:14,380
You lost sight of the thing that made you successful to begin with.

100
00:08:14,380 --> 00:08:21,940
The thing that made you successful to begin with was sitting in the love of God and allowing the love of God to pour out from you and through you.

101
00:08:21,940 --> 00:08:31,020
So, I think Paul is saying here, look, success in the Christian life comes from looking to Jesus, following the example of Jesus.

102
00:08:31,020 --> 00:08:35,660
He's going to say, having the same mind of Christ Jesus.

103
00:08:35,660 --> 00:08:39,140
And then he's going to unpack this idea of humility.

104
00:08:39,140 --> 00:08:41,880
So that's the outlines of this morning.

105
00:08:41,880 --> 00:08:42,740
That's the direction we're going.

106
00:08:42,740 --> 00:08:43,720
Does that sound good with everybody?

107
00:08:44,380 --> 00:08:46,860
We're nine minutes in, and that's the intro.

108
00:08:46,860 --> 00:08:52,560
So let me pray, and then I'll try and keep close to my promise, so I'll give you a few minutes back.

109
00:08:52,560 --> 00:09:08,380
Father, we are so grateful for the example of Jesus, for the humility of Jesus, for the way in which he disregarded all of his power and authority and position.

110
00:09:08,380 --> 00:09:23,920
And then, as God and creator of God, to come and be born a human being in the first century in a small town that most people have never heard of before.

111
00:09:24,420 --> 00:09:40,660
To live a human being in the first century in a small town that most people have never heard of him being in the first century in a small town that most people have never heard of before.

112
00:09:40,660 --> 00:09:58,200
And for the people in the first century in a small town that most people have never heard of him being in the first century in a small town that most people have never heard of him being in the first century in a small town that most people have never heard of before.

113
00:09:58,200 --> 00:10:16,340
And until that day comes, God, you've called us to have the same mind, the same attitude as Christ Jesus, who didn't think about himself more highly than he ought to, but he laid himself down.

114
00:10:16,440 --> 00:10:20,300
We have the same mind that we think of others as more important than ourselves.

115
00:10:20,300 --> 00:10:23,260
And so, God, this is not something that comes naturally to us.

116
00:10:23,260 --> 00:10:28,740
We need your Holy Spirit to challenge, convict, encourage, point us to you this morning.

117
00:10:28,740 --> 00:10:31,320
Do that this morning for your glory and for our good.

118
00:10:31,320 --> 00:10:33,160
In Jesus' name I pray.

119
00:10:33,160 --> 00:10:34,060
Amen.

120
00:10:35,920 --> 00:10:40,920
Well, in a divided society, I don't know if you guys know this about our society divided.

121
00:10:40,920 --> 00:10:43,680
There's lots of arguments about anything and everything.

122
00:10:43,680 --> 00:10:54,520
I think we can almost all agree on one simple thing, and that is this, that assembling things that you buy is the worst.

123
00:10:54,520 --> 00:11:03,140
Like, if you pay money for something and then you have to put it together yourself, that's like one of the absolute worst things that could ever happen.

124
00:11:03,140 --> 00:11:09,860
And the last thing I had to assemble, my wife and I, we got a treadmill with stimulus money, you know?

125
00:11:09,860 --> 00:11:11,600
I was just trying to stimulate the economy.

126
00:11:11,600 --> 00:11:17,840
I was stuck in my house, so I bought a treadmill with the stimulus money, and, you know, it was just great all around.

127
00:11:17,840 --> 00:11:24,420
But I guess I never had a treadmill before, and whenever you see treadmills, they just, like, exist like a treadmill.

128
00:11:24,420 --> 00:11:30,060
So I thought, you buy a treadmill, you pick up a treadmill, you take it to your house, you put it in your house, it's a treadmill.

129
00:11:30,060 --> 00:11:31,420
No, it has to be put together.

130
00:11:32,540 --> 00:11:38,340
And to be honest, it's the closest to a divorce that I have ever come, setting up this treadmill.

131
00:11:38,340 --> 00:11:45,540
I probably could have used a sermon on humility before, during, and after the process of setting up this treadmill.

132
00:11:45,540 --> 00:11:47,100
It was difficult.

133
00:11:47,100 --> 00:11:50,320
But we finally got it, like, seven or eight hours later.

134
00:11:50,320 --> 00:11:52,200
It was midnight when we finished the treadmill.

135
00:11:52,300 --> 00:11:54,340
And it's still not together, as it should be.

136
00:11:54,340 --> 00:11:56,940
It's together enough that it works.

137
00:11:56,940 --> 00:12:02,100
But there's some screws that are in places that they weren't supposed to be in, but they were already there.

138
00:12:02,100 --> 00:12:03,480
And it was, like, step three.

139
00:12:03,480 --> 00:12:05,480
And we were on step seven when we realized.

140
00:12:05,480 --> 00:12:06,800
So, anyway, we finished it.

141
00:12:06,800 --> 00:12:07,500
We made it happen.

142
00:12:08,300 --> 00:12:16,040
But, if I'm honest, the one thing, the one thing that makes assembling things better now for me, I'm not handy at all, very bad at putting things together.

143
00:12:16,040 --> 00:12:17,720
But I'm a visual learner.

144
00:12:17,840 --> 00:12:21,680
So, nowadays, YouTube has, like, a tutorial video for everything.

145
00:12:21,680 --> 00:12:23,520
Like, I don't know.

146
00:12:23,520 --> 00:12:24,820
It's incredible.

147
00:12:24,820 --> 00:12:25,680
Literally everything.

148
00:12:25,680 --> 00:12:33,600
You type in any product on YouTube, and there's a tutorial video of somebody that has the product, holding the product, showing you how to make it work.

149
00:12:33,600 --> 00:12:38,900
And so, that has a little bit alleviated the stress for me of assembling things.

150
00:12:38,900 --> 00:12:47,560
And the reason that I share this story with you as we hop into Philippians chapter 2 is because Paul kind of draws out this idea of humility.

151
00:12:47,560 --> 00:12:51,560
I think he kind of lays it out at the beginning with some general instructions.

152
00:12:51,560 --> 00:12:54,480
Like, on paper, this is what humility looks like.

153
00:12:54,480 --> 00:12:56,640
This is what you're supposed to do to be humble.

154
00:12:56,640 --> 00:13:05,420
But then, just in case we would be confused, just in case we might get it wrong, he gives us kind of a tutorial video or a vivid picture of Christ doing it.

155
00:13:05,420 --> 00:13:10,180
He shows us what it looks like to be humble by showing us a picture of Jesus.

156
00:13:10,180 --> 00:13:13,940
And then, at the end, he tells us what the final product looks like.

157
00:13:13,940 --> 00:13:18,520
When true humility is lived out, what is the end result?

158
00:13:18,520 --> 00:13:22,600
There's nothing better than standing back and seeing the final product.

159
00:13:22,600 --> 00:13:30,020
And so, as we unpack this statement that the successful Christian life and church are assembled with an attitude of humility,

160
00:13:30,020 --> 00:13:32,460
we're going to break it down into those three parts.

161
00:13:32,460 --> 00:13:39,360
It's the general instructions, the video tutorial or the picture of what that looks like, and then the end result.

162
00:13:39,360 --> 00:13:44,500
So, the general instructions are found in chapter 2, verses 3 and 4.

163
00:13:44,500 --> 00:13:45,860
Paul says,

164
00:13:45,860 --> 00:14:04,620
Pretty basic, pretty simple instructions.

165
00:14:04,840 --> 00:14:12,040
This is what Paul says, that those of you who are going to assemble your life with an attitude of humility, this is what your life should look like.

166
00:14:12,040 --> 00:14:12,760
Don't be selfish.

167
00:14:12,760 --> 00:14:14,540
Don't try to impress others.

168
00:14:14,540 --> 00:14:15,060
Be humble.

169
00:14:15,060 --> 00:14:19,300
Thinking of others as better than yourselves.

170
00:14:19,300 --> 00:14:23,280
It's a pretty simple instruction, pretty basic instruction.

171
00:14:23,860 --> 00:14:29,680
But, I mean, even when you assemble things, like, the engineers do a pretty good job of making the instructions as simple as possible,

172
00:14:29,680 --> 00:14:33,160
but it's easy to read into things and question things.

173
00:14:33,160 --> 00:14:36,940
And you're like, wait a second, does that mean this goes here and that goes there?

174
00:14:36,940 --> 00:14:39,760
And I think the same can happen with our own prideful, selfish hearts.

175
00:14:39,760 --> 00:14:41,340
We hear things like, don't be selfish.

176
00:14:41,340 --> 00:14:42,880
Don't try and impress others.

177
00:14:42,880 --> 00:14:43,980
And we're like, yeah, I get that.

178
00:14:44,060 --> 00:14:46,220
But, what about in this situation?

179
00:14:46,220 --> 00:14:47,880
Or what about in this circumstance?

180
00:14:47,880 --> 00:14:49,600
Or what if this happens?

181
00:14:49,600 --> 00:14:52,720
And we tend to get distracted by the basic instructions.

182
00:14:52,720 --> 00:14:59,540
Essentially what Paul is saying here is he's saying, don't selfishly serve your own interests,

183
00:14:59,540 --> 00:15:04,640
but instead selflessly serve the interests of others all the time.

184
00:15:04,640 --> 00:15:06,080
That's the command.

185
00:15:06,080 --> 00:15:09,460
That's how we live out this attitude of humility.

186
00:15:09,460 --> 00:15:17,740
Don't selfishly serve your own interests, but instead selflessly serve the interests of others.

187
00:15:17,740 --> 00:15:21,520
See, I think most of us, we like this idea.

188
00:15:21,520 --> 00:15:23,040
We would agree with this attitude.

189
00:15:23,040 --> 00:15:24,940
We like the idea of selflessly serving others.

190
00:15:24,940 --> 00:15:28,160
We've all heard it said that it's better to give than to receive.

191
00:15:28,160 --> 00:15:35,800
I think we all like the idea of selflessly serving others as long as it fits nicely into a few hours of our calendar

192
00:15:35,800 --> 00:15:40,980
in between the majority of tasks that ultimately revolve around us.

193
00:15:40,980 --> 00:15:41,900
Right?

194
00:15:41,900 --> 00:15:44,820
Like selflessly, sacrificially serving other people.

195
00:15:44,820 --> 00:15:51,000
We like the idea if it fits in a schedule that ultimately is built around our desires, our wants, our needs,

196
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:54,080
our family's desires, our family's wants, and their needs.

197
00:15:55,800 --> 00:16:04,100
And if we're really honest, like we get like super transparent, sometimes even our willingness to serve others comes out of the selfish desire.

198
00:16:04,100 --> 00:16:10,960
Even our own humility, like we feign humility in order to build this reputation where people think we're awesome,

199
00:16:10,960 --> 00:16:14,980
where people think we're successful, where people think we're really crushing it as a Christian

200
00:16:14,980 --> 00:16:18,320
because we selflessly sacrifice and serve others all the time.

201
00:16:18,320 --> 00:16:24,580
And sometimes if we're really honest, the motivation of our hearts to do that is so that other people will look at us and think we're great.

202
00:16:25,800 --> 00:16:31,240
Which is not the reason that Jesus came to humbly sacrificially serve us.

203
00:16:31,240 --> 00:16:41,900
And this is the primary invitation, the general direction for a humble life is to not try to impress others,

204
00:16:41,900 --> 00:16:44,240
but to humbly serve others.

205
00:16:44,240 --> 00:16:51,240
And I just think humility is one of those things that like has to actively, intentionally be pursued every day.

206
00:16:51,240 --> 00:16:53,400
It's a lifelong process.

207
00:16:53,400 --> 00:16:55,520
We never get good at humility.

208
00:16:55,520 --> 00:17:03,500
Like it's just we get a little bit better at it day in and day out, but we have to prioritize it because if not, it flips away.

209
00:17:04,020 --> 00:17:09,580
I was listening to a sermon this week about humility and the guy pointed out something that was super convicting.

210
00:17:09,580 --> 00:17:11,220
I just love being convicted.

211
00:17:11,220 --> 00:17:12,300
Don't you guys love being convicted?

212
00:17:12,300 --> 00:17:17,680
He said, maybe you're not sure if you really struggle with this whole pride thing.

213
00:17:17,680 --> 00:17:20,920
He said, if you're ever in a room, like in a party, in a group of people,

214
00:17:20,920 --> 00:17:27,000
and you notice that somebody else is trying to draw attention to themselves, and you're frustrated by that in any way,

215
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:28,860
you're probably prideful.

216
00:17:28,860 --> 00:17:33,220
He's like, you're probably thinking, why does he or she get to be the center of attention?

217
00:17:33,220 --> 00:17:36,740
Can't everybody tell that they're telling a one-up everybody else's stories?

218
00:17:36,740 --> 00:17:39,060
They're trying to make this thing about them.

219
00:17:39,060 --> 00:17:41,260
And humble people don't think that way.

220
00:17:41,700 --> 00:17:46,320
Because humble people are too busy trying to sacrificially serve other people, but prideful people pay attention.

221
00:17:46,320 --> 00:17:48,080
And they say, that guy's being prideful.

222
00:17:48,080 --> 00:17:49,400
That girl's being prideful.

223
00:17:49,400 --> 00:17:53,220
She's trying to be the center of attention when in reality, you're just frustrated.

224
00:17:53,220 --> 00:17:57,580
I'm just frustrated that I'm not the center of attention.

225
00:17:57,580 --> 00:18:04,780
So we're called to humbly, sacrificially serve other people, to treat other people better than ourselves,

226
00:18:04,780 --> 00:18:05,900
to not try and impress people.

227
00:18:05,900 --> 00:18:10,500
What are the core attitudes or ideas that get in the way of this?

228
00:18:10,720 --> 00:18:14,020
Like, what gets in the way of true humility?

229
00:18:14,020 --> 00:18:22,160
Well, I think as I was wrestling with this, I came up with three general ideas that I think really get in the way of humility.

230
00:18:22,160 --> 00:18:28,860
And Jesus calls them out in his life when he's interacting with his disciples and with other people that are seeking to follow him.

231
00:18:28,860 --> 00:18:30,260
The first, I would say, is wealth.

232
00:18:30,260 --> 00:18:35,420
Wealth often gets in the way of humbly, sacrificially serving other people.

233
00:18:35,420 --> 00:18:41,260
There's a guy that's interested in following Jesus in Mark chapter 10, verses 21 through 23.

234
00:18:41,260 --> 00:18:45,880
And he comes and he asks Jesus, like, what does it look like to follow God?

235
00:18:45,880 --> 00:18:49,080
And he's like, hey, love the Lord your God with all your heart, soul, mind, and strength.

236
00:18:49,080 --> 00:18:50,340
Love your neighbor as yourself.

237
00:18:50,340 --> 00:18:51,680
And the guy's like, dude, I'd do that.

238
00:18:51,680 --> 00:18:53,200
Jesus is like, great.

239
00:18:53,700 --> 00:18:58,580
And he says this, looking at the man, Jesus felt genuine love for him.

240
00:18:58,580 --> 00:19:02,380
And he says this, there's still one thing that you haven't done.

241
00:19:02,380 --> 00:19:08,920
Go and sell all of your possessions and give the money to the poor and you will have treasures in heaven.

242
00:19:09,080 --> 00:19:10,840
Then come and follow me.

243
00:19:10,840 --> 00:19:16,880
At this, the man's face fell and he went away sad, for he had many possessions.

244
00:19:16,880 --> 00:19:23,220
Jesus looked around and said to his disciples, how hard is it for the rich man to enter the kingdom of God?

245
00:19:23,220 --> 00:19:30,780
See, one major obstacle to building, to assembling our lives with an attitude of humility is his pursuit of wealth.

246
00:19:32,200 --> 00:19:42,660
And often it's done out of an attitude of self-preservation, to build up our own kingdom, to have enough, to just provide for ourselves in a way that makes us feel comfortable,

247
00:19:42,660 --> 00:19:46,680
in a way that we don't have to fully rely or trust on God.

248
00:19:46,680 --> 00:19:55,580
We build up extra wealth that could be used to serve others, but it makes us comfortable sitting in our savings accounts.

249
00:19:55,580 --> 00:19:57,980
And that's what this guy was struggling with.

250
00:19:57,980 --> 00:20:00,120
He had far more than enough than he needed.

251
00:20:00,840 --> 00:20:03,140
And Jesus said, this is an obstacle for you.

252
00:20:03,140 --> 00:20:06,640
This is keeping you from genuinely following me.

253
00:20:06,640 --> 00:20:10,840
This is keeping you from humbly, sacrificially serving other people.

254
00:20:10,840 --> 00:20:11,880
And here's the thing with wealth.

255
00:20:11,880 --> 00:20:16,100
Like, just being wealthy isn't in and of itself a sin.

256
00:20:16,100 --> 00:20:20,060
And having wealth isn't in and of itself going to keep you from being humble.

257
00:20:20,060 --> 00:20:27,780
I have met many people that are very wealthy, but that use their wealth as a resource to sacrificially serve other people.

258
00:20:27,900 --> 00:20:31,000
It can be a tool to be humble.

259
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:34,020
It can be a tool to sacrificially serve other people.

260
00:20:34,020 --> 00:20:39,780
But it can also be a thing that we pursue with all of our time and energy and resources.

261
00:20:39,780 --> 00:20:45,840
We're far too concerned about creating side hustle jobs to get more and more money for ourselves, to store up more and more,

262
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:50,080
when in reality, like, all the attitude that comes from that is just, how can I get more?

263
00:20:50,080 --> 00:20:52,140
How can I serve myself more?

264
00:20:52,140 --> 00:20:54,940
And this gets a humility.

265
00:20:54,940 --> 00:21:01,720
The second obstacle is reputation, status, like having people think that we're awesome.

266
00:21:01,720 --> 00:21:06,440
In Jesus' disciples in Mark chapter 10, verses 35 to 38, it says,

267
00:21:06,440 --> 00:21:11,140
Then James and John, the sons of Zebedee, they came over and they spoke to Jesus.

268
00:21:11,140 --> 00:21:13,820
They said, Teacher, we want you to do us a favor.

269
00:21:13,820 --> 00:21:18,460
Have you guys ever come to Jesus and said, Can you do me a favor?

270
00:21:18,460 --> 00:21:19,500
Have you ever been there?

271
00:21:19,500 --> 00:21:20,880
Maybe.

272
00:21:20,880 --> 00:21:22,480
I have a couple times.

273
00:21:22,480 --> 00:21:23,820
We want you to do us a favor.

274
00:21:23,820 --> 00:21:25,080
Jesus said, What is your request?

275
00:21:25,140 --> 00:21:30,800
They replied, When you sit in your glorious throne, we want to sit in the places of honor next to you,

276
00:21:30,800 --> 00:21:33,420
one on your right and one on your left.

277
00:21:33,420 --> 00:21:38,260
The other ten disciples found out about this, and they were mad.

278
00:21:38,260 --> 00:21:44,740
So Jesus called them all together, and he said, The rulers of this world, they lorded over their people.

279
00:21:44,740 --> 00:21:53,220
They used their status, their reputation as a leader to inflict this negative consequence on other people.

280
00:21:53,780 --> 00:21:58,240
Officials, they flaunt their authority over those under them.

281
00:21:58,240 --> 00:22:04,860
But among you, among Christians, among followers of Jesus, among those people of the kingdom of God, it will be different.

282
00:22:04,860 --> 00:22:12,920
Whoever wants to be a leader among you must be your servant, and whoever wants to be first among you must be the slave of everyone else.

283
00:22:12,920 --> 00:22:20,640
For even the Son of Man came not to be served, but to serve others and to give his life a ransom for many.

284
00:22:22,420 --> 00:22:25,320
So often, we don't sacrificially serve others.

285
00:22:25,320 --> 00:22:29,620
We don't have humility because we're trying to build up a reputation for ourselves.

286
00:22:29,620 --> 00:22:35,080
We use our time and our energy and our resources and our interactions with other people to puff ourselves up,

287
00:22:35,080 --> 00:22:38,800
to try and create this persona where people think we're great.

288
00:22:38,800 --> 00:22:40,320
And this is what James and John were doing.

289
00:22:40,320 --> 00:22:41,300
They were following Jesus.

290
00:22:41,300 --> 00:22:42,560
They were doing the right thing, right?

291
00:22:42,620 --> 00:22:49,760
Like, they gave up a lot to follow Jesus, but then they got distracted and they thought, Jesus is famous, and we want to be famous, too.

292
00:22:49,760 --> 00:22:53,360
Like, we want this status that comes with being famous.

293
00:22:53,360 --> 00:22:54,460
And we gave up so much.

294
00:22:54,460 --> 00:22:55,660
We deserve this.

295
00:22:57,120 --> 00:23:01,080
How often do we fall into the same trap, trying to build a reputation for ourselves?

296
00:23:01,080 --> 00:23:02,680
We gave up a lot to follow Jesus.

297
00:23:02,680 --> 00:23:08,500
It would be nice if I had this great reputation of people around me, if people thought I was an awesome Christian,

298
00:23:08,500 --> 00:23:16,620
if people thought I was a great mom, a great dad, if I had this status where people looked at me and wanted my wisdom and my resources.

299
00:23:16,920 --> 00:23:20,740
Reputation gets in the way of humility.

300
00:23:20,740 --> 00:23:27,120
I found out this week as I was studying that the most asked question of Jesus in the Gospels is, who is the greatest?

301
00:23:27,120 --> 00:23:30,400
Like, people just kept asking this question.

302
00:23:30,400 --> 00:23:35,560
Reputation is something that's at the central idea of our hearts.

303
00:23:35,560 --> 00:23:44,100
And Jesus attacked the attitude that his disciples had about reputation in the Lord's Supper, in John chapter 13.

304
00:23:44,780 --> 00:23:48,440
See, Jesus was in charge of creating the preparations for the Last Supper.

305
00:23:48,440 --> 00:23:50,520
He sent his disciples ahead.

306
00:23:50,520 --> 00:23:51,800
He's like, hey, I've got it taken care of.

307
00:23:51,800 --> 00:23:52,500
Go to this place.

308
00:23:52,500 --> 00:23:54,160
They'll take you up to this room.

309
00:23:54,160 --> 00:23:54,760
Take care of it.

310
00:23:54,760 --> 00:23:55,340
Set it all up.

311
00:23:55,340 --> 00:23:55,500
Right?

312
00:23:55,500 --> 00:24:04,740
And the person who was in charge of setting something up, like setting that meal up, would have also been in charge of getting a servant to wash the feet of the people that walked in for the meal.

313
00:24:04,740 --> 00:24:07,380
Jesus intentionally didn't do that.

314
00:24:07,380 --> 00:24:11,080
Because he wanted to see what his followers would do.

315
00:24:11,080 --> 00:24:14,820
They'd been with him for three years, washing his life.

316
00:24:14,820 --> 00:24:19,220
He wanted to see how they learned their lessons.

317
00:24:19,220 --> 00:24:24,680
They understand that this thing is all about humble sacrifice, selflessly serving other people.

318
00:24:27,020 --> 00:24:39,560
They file in one by one, jockeying for the best seat of the table, trying to make sure that the other disciples knew that Jesus liked them better, that they were more successful at this discipling thing.

319
00:24:41,300 --> 00:24:55,380
And as they were jockeying for positions, trying to build reputation and status for themselves, the creator of the universe got down on his hands and knees and started to wash their feet.

320
00:24:58,900 --> 00:25:05,560
Looked them in the eye, covered in dust, dirt, feces, washed their feet.

321
00:25:05,560 --> 00:25:12,760
And then he gets to Peter, and all of us love Peter, right?

322
00:25:12,760 --> 00:25:16,080
Because he makes us feel better about ourselves sometimes.

323
00:25:16,080 --> 00:25:20,320
And he's washing Peter's feet, and Peter goes, no, wash everything.

324
00:25:20,320 --> 00:25:21,100
Give me a bath.

325
00:25:21,860 --> 00:25:26,020
And I think the attitude there is this false humility, this false reputation, right?

326
00:25:26,020 --> 00:25:28,660
It's like, let me be, I'm so good at this thing.

327
00:25:28,660 --> 00:25:40,120
Like, man, we just get so distracted by trying to build status and reputation for ourselves when the one that we're following gets down on his hands and knees to wash his feet.

328
00:25:40,120 --> 00:25:43,800
The last thing that distracts us from humility is strength.

329
00:25:46,000 --> 00:25:48,060
Jesus is about to go to the cross.

330
00:25:48,060 --> 00:25:48,740
He gets arrested.

331
00:25:48,740 --> 00:25:53,100
And in Mark 14, 46 and 47, it says, the others grabbed Jesus and arrested him.

332
00:25:53,100 --> 00:25:57,140
But one man, again, Peter, thanks for making us feel better about ourselves.

333
00:25:57,140 --> 00:26:04,680
He pulled out his sword, and he struck the high priest's sleigh, flashing off his ear.

334
00:26:10,220 --> 00:26:15,640
We try and overcome this attitude of selflessly sacrificing.

335
00:26:15,640 --> 00:26:23,020
Jesus is on his way to selflessly sacrifice for you, for me, for his disciples, for the people who are arresting him.

336
00:26:23,020 --> 00:26:25,180
It's all about humble sacrifice.

337
00:26:25,180 --> 00:26:26,940
And instead, we're like, no, this shouldn't happen.

338
00:26:26,940 --> 00:26:28,220
We need to take control.

339
00:26:28,220 --> 00:26:36,660
We need to use our strength, our power, our resources, whatever we have to fight back against rather than to selflessly serve.

340
00:26:37,460 --> 00:26:46,780
And Jesus, time and time again, sacrificially serves, lays down his privileges, his resources, his life to serve us.

341
00:26:46,780 --> 00:26:49,340
I love this quote from F.B. Meyer.

342
00:26:49,340 --> 00:26:53,680
He says, I used to think that God's gifts were on shelves, one above another.

343
00:26:53,680 --> 00:26:57,820
As the taller we grow, the easier we can reach them.

344
00:26:57,820 --> 00:27:04,020
But now I find that God's gifts are on shelves, and the lower we stoop, the more that we get.

345
00:27:05,600 --> 00:27:10,640
See, the disciples, the ones that gave up everything to follow Jesus, they're in the position that we are, right?

346
00:27:10,640 --> 00:27:21,300
Like, the decision that we made, for those of you that are followers of Jesus, like, you decided to give up your own privileges, your own desires, your own wants, and your own needs to follow after Jesus.

347
00:27:21,300 --> 00:27:22,980
You're in the same position as the disciples.

348
00:27:22,980 --> 00:27:27,680
And as they were following Jesus, they got distracted, and they thought, let me get better at this thing.

349
00:27:27,680 --> 00:27:29,180
Let me grow up higher at this thing.

350
00:27:29,180 --> 00:27:30,820
Let me climb this ladder.

351
00:27:30,820 --> 00:27:34,640
And Jesus constantly pushed them to walk down the ladder, not up the ladder.

352
00:27:34,640 --> 00:27:37,580
And it flies in the face of the American ideology.

353
00:27:37,580 --> 00:27:43,040
The American ideology is all about climbing the ladder of wealth, status, and strength and power.

354
00:27:43,040 --> 00:27:44,520
It's all about climbing up.

355
00:27:44,520 --> 00:27:47,860
And the kingdom of God is all about climbing down.

356
00:27:47,860 --> 00:27:52,400
We naturally don't congregate at the bottom of the shelf.

357
00:27:52,520 --> 00:27:56,820
We tend to reject humility, or we fake humility to climb the ladder.

358
00:27:56,820 --> 00:28:05,580
Paul's instructions for successful Christian life and church go directly in the face of some of our deepest desires for wealth and status and strength.

359
00:28:08,580 --> 00:28:16,540
So just in case we were going to get confused by Paul's instructions and make excuses for not always sacrificially serving others,

360
00:28:16,540 --> 00:28:21,100
he gives us this picture, this tutorial video of Jesus doing it perfectly.

361
00:28:21,100 --> 00:28:22,280
Verses 5 through 8.

362
00:28:22,280 --> 00:28:24,160
It says,

363
00:28:24,320 --> 00:28:39,020
This passage preaches for itself.

364
00:28:39,020 --> 00:28:40,200
I'm going to try and stay all the way.

365
00:28:40,200 --> 00:28:42,400
Instead, he gave up.

366
00:28:42,400 --> 00:28:44,540
Jesus gave up his divine privileges.

367
00:28:44,540 --> 00:28:48,280
He took a humble position of a slave and was born a human being.

368
00:28:48,280 --> 00:28:56,580
And when he appeared in human form, he humbled himself in obedience to God and died a criminal's death on the cross.

369
00:28:56,580 --> 00:28:57,660
Paul says,

370
00:28:57,660 --> 00:28:59,040
Do you want to know what humility looks like?

371
00:28:59,040 --> 00:29:02,200
Do you want to know how to live out humility perfectly?

372
00:29:02,200 --> 00:29:03,860
Do what Jesus did.

373
00:29:03,860 --> 00:29:06,760
Have the same mind that Jesus had.

374
00:29:06,760 --> 00:29:13,000
If we're going to grow in the attitude of humility, it starts and ends with daily looking at Jesus.

375
00:29:16,680 --> 00:29:24,360
Essentially, these are lyrics of a worship song that the early church was singing to remind themselves of all that Jesus had done for them,

376
00:29:24,360 --> 00:29:28,060
of his humility from his birth to his exaltation.

377
00:29:28,060 --> 00:29:30,880
See, I love where this starts.

378
00:29:30,880 --> 00:29:33,200
It starts with his incarnation.

379
00:29:33,200 --> 00:29:34,220
It starts with his birth.

380
00:29:34,220 --> 00:29:40,300
And honestly, most of us, as we think about the humility of Jesus, we tend to rush to the cross.

381
00:29:40,300 --> 00:29:43,640
Like 95% of the year we think about the cross, except for like December.

382
00:29:43,640 --> 00:29:45,500
And then we think about baby Jesus in the manger.

383
00:29:45,500 --> 00:29:49,780
December, and when we watch, what's that movie?

384
00:29:49,780 --> 00:29:52,100
Will Ferrell, baby Jesus.

385
00:29:52,100 --> 00:29:53,780
Oh, they're good nights.

386
00:29:53,780 --> 00:29:54,720
We wouldn't do that, though.

387
00:29:54,720 --> 00:29:55,600
We would not do that.

388
00:29:55,600 --> 00:29:57,000
None of us have ever done that.

389
00:29:57,000 --> 00:29:57,280
Okay.

390
00:29:57,280 --> 00:30:03,520
We tend to rush to the cross, but Paul's passage here starts with the incarnation.

391
00:30:03,520 --> 00:30:08,520
Coming to earth to be born of a human was all about sacrificial service.

392
00:30:08,520 --> 00:30:10,020
It was all about humility.

393
00:30:10,020 --> 00:30:13,220
Jesus came in a little-known town called Bethlehem.

394
00:30:13,220 --> 00:30:15,620
He sacrificed everything.

395
00:30:15,620 --> 00:30:19,600
He laid down his equality with God to be born a human being.

396
00:30:19,600 --> 00:30:22,340
Diapers, learning to walk, learning to talk.

397
00:30:22,340 --> 00:30:24,900
The creator of the universe went through puberty.

398
00:30:24,900 --> 00:30:26,420
Puberty!

399
00:30:26,420 --> 00:30:28,620
For you and for me.

400
00:30:28,620 --> 00:30:30,300
That's humility.

401
00:30:30,300 --> 00:30:32,300
That's sacrifice.

402
00:30:32,300 --> 00:30:33,160
His incarnation.

403
00:30:33,160 --> 00:30:34,600
Humility.

404
00:30:35,020 --> 00:30:48,880
Every moment of his life, from his incarnation to his brutal death on the cross, and let's not lose track of the sight that Romans didn't kill people on the cross because it was effective, because it was efficient, because it was a cost-effective way to kill people.

405
00:30:48,880 --> 00:30:51,860
They did it to humiliate the person being killed.

406
00:30:51,860 --> 00:31:03,220
It's the most humiliating death that anybody could ever have experienced, and from his incarnation, from his birth and his life to his death on the Roman cross, it was all humility.

407
00:31:04,120 --> 00:31:07,240
He did it to serve you and to serve me.

408
00:31:07,240 --> 00:31:10,820
I love how Andrew Murray puts it.

409
00:31:10,820 --> 00:31:11,200
He says,

410
00:31:11,200 --> 00:31:18,580
Humility is the chief characteristic that is the root and essence of Christ's character as our Redeemer.

411
00:31:18,580 --> 00:31:21,960
What is his incarnation but his heavenly humility?

412
00:31:21,960 --> 00:31:25,300
What is his life but his earthly humility?

413
00:31:25,300 --> 00:31:28,020
And what is his atonement but humility?

414
00:31:28,820 --> 00:31:31,480
He humbled himself and became obedient to death.

415
00:31:31,480 --> 00:31:35,500
And what is his ascension and his glory but humility exalted?

416
00:31:35,500 --> 00:31:37,240
He humbled himself.

417
00:31:37,240 --> 00:31:48,360
Therefore, God exalted him to the highest place in heaven where he was one with the Father in his birth, in his life, and in his death on the earth, and in his return to the right hand of the Father.

418
00:31:48,500 --> 00:31:50,740
It is all humility.

419
00:31:50,740 --> 00:32:02,140
From his birth, to his life, to his death, to his resurrection and ascension, it was all about sacrificially loving and serving us.

420
00:32:03,220 --> 00:32:10,380
And notice how in this passage, Jesus gives up the three things that we tend to struggle with when it comes to humility.

421
00:32:10,380 --> 00:32:12,880
He gives up his reputation in verse 6.

422
00:32:12,980 --> 00:32:17,440
Though he was God, he did not think of equality with God as something to cling to.

423
00:32:17,440 --> 00:32:23,720
He gave up his status at sitting at the right hand of the Father as creator of the universe.

424
00:32:23,720 --> 00:32:35,980
He literally gave up his reputation to be born a no-name, a carpenter that nobody knew or cared about, to live 30 years of his life in obscurity, humility.

425
00:32:35,980 --> 00:32:37,580
He gave up his reputation.

426
00:32:37,580 --> 00:32:39,100
He gave up his wealth.

427
00:32:39,100 --> 00:32:42,360
In verse 7, instead, he gave up his divine privileges.

428
00:32:42,360 --> 00:32:48,440
